{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790429220,"nonce":"/GZAPRjUYnrxofUqFXoWmJDUo5Q=","title":"wasm: dynamic calls into Haxe through Ash's per-signature trampolines","message":"caribou-ash's typed dispatch calls a compiled Haxe function through\nash_native_call::dispatch_by_pattern. That passes every integer as an\ni64 and transmutes the address to the matching Rust signature. wasm\ntype-checks each call_indirect, so a callee taking an i32 or a pointer\n(i32 on wasm32) traps. Ash's AOT emits one trampoline per function type\nin the program, `(fun, args, out)` and keyed by the hl_type\n(crates/ash/src/llvm/aot_trampoline.rs), because a call assembled at run\ntime is impossible on wasm.\n\nAt AOT, a typed cross-language site links statically (linking.md), so\nthe bridge only carries untyped members and dynamic sends. On wasm\nthose must use Ash's trampolines: look one up by the callee's hl_type\ninstead of dispatch_by_pattern. The same holds for any other language's\ntyped callable reached dynamically.","files":null}]}